cham3-desktop for Debian
------------------------

M1 visual and package foundation. Everything shipped here is presentation.

What is installed
  /usr/share/plasma/plasmoids/org.cham3.*            eight panel plasmoids
  /usr/share/plasma/look-and-feel/org.cham3.desktop  look-and-feel package
  /usr/share/plasma/layout-templates/org.cham3.panel right-edge panel layout
  /usr/share/color-schemes/Cham3Dark.colors          color scheme
  /usr/share/wallpapers/Cham3                        wallpaper
  /usr/share/cham3/branding                          logos
  /etc/xdg/kdeglobals, /etc/xdg/plasmarc             system-wide default
        look-and-feel, color scheme and icon theme (lowest precedence; any
        user setting overrides them)

What is not here
  No contract, identity, vault, sandbox, network or kill-switch backend
  exists in M1. The panel widgets report those features as unavailable and
  perform no security action. No .desktop launchers are shipped for
  applications that are not installed.

Wayland
  The cham3 session is KDE Plasma on Wayland (plasma-workspace provides the
  "plasma.desktop" Wayland session, kwin-wayland provides the compositor).

Panel layout
  The default panel is applied by plasmashell on the first start of a new
  user profile. An existing profile keeps its own panel configuration; apply
  the layout to an existing profile from System Settings, Appearance, Global
  Theme, choosing "cham3" with the option to reset the desktop layout.

Packet capture (dumpcap)
  Wireshark's capture helper /usr/bin/dumpcap (package wireshark-common, pulled
  in by cham3-full) must never run as set-user-id root. Debian's
  wireshark-common gives non-root capture through the "wireshark" group plus
  the file capabilities cap_net_admin and cap_net_raw, but falls back to a
  set-user-id root dumpcap when setcap is missing or fails. cham3-desktop
  removes that fallback:
    - debian/postinst pins a dpkg-statoverride (owner root, group root, mode
      0750) on dumpcap.
      While an override exists, wireshark-common's postinst changes nothing on
      the file, so the fallback cannot run on an upgrade, on dpkg-reconfigure
      wireshark-common, or when wireshark-common is installed after
      cham3-desktop. dpkg applies the override on every unpack, so a new
      dumpcap is never set-user-id either.
    - the same script runs when cham3-desktop is configured and, through a dpkg
      file trigger on /usr/bin/dumpcap, after any package installs, upgrades or
      removes that file. It clears the set-user-id and set-group-id bits, makes
      the file root:wireshark mode 0750 (no access for others) and sets
      cap_net_admin,cap_net_raw=eip.
    - if the capabilities cannot be set, for example on a file system without
      capability support, dumpcap is left without any set-id bit and without
      capabilities: capture by non-root users is unavailable until they can be
      set (fix the cause, then run: dpkg-reconfigure cham3-desktop). The
      failure is reported on standard error.
  The override names group root, not "wireshark", because wireshark-common
  deletes the wireshark group when it is purged and dpkg aborts on an override
  naming an unknown group. The postrm removes the override when cham3-desktop
  is removed, but only if it is exactly this one. An override set by the
  administrator is respected: only the set-id bits are cleared, and
  capabilities are set only if the file is root:wireshark with no access for
  others.
  Membership of the "wireshark" group is NOT granted by installation. Adding a
  user to the group is a deliberate administrative step that lets that user
  capture the network traffic visible to the host, so treat it as a privilege.
  Once the override exists, the wireshark-common debconf answer install-setuid
  (preseeded true in the image) no longer affects dumpcap, and
  dpkg-reconfigure wireshark-common leaves the file as it is, including any
  capabilities that were set earlier. Run dpkg-reconfigure cham3-desktop to
  apply the policy again.
  Limits: this covers the file /usr/bin/dumpcap only. In the one dpkg run that
  installs wireshark-common and cham3-desktop together for the first time,
  Debian's own postinst can still set the bit if dpkg configures
  wireshark-common first; cham3-desktop clears it when it is configured later
  in the same run, so the end state is safe but the interval is not zero.
  Whether file capabilities survive the squashfs image and the installer's
  copy to disk is a property of that tooling and has to be checked on the
  built image; the package tests do not cover it.
